    "https://wustl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Washington-University-Danforth-Campus/Manager-WashU-BearLine--Direct-Marketing---Participation---University-Advancement_JR97171": {
      "title": "Manager WashU BearLine, Direct Marketing & Participation - University Advancement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nLocation ST. LOUIS, MO 63130&#xa;&#xa; Scheduled Hours 37.5 Position Summary The Manager WashU BearLine, Direct Marketing & Participation (student calling program) will support the execution of engagement-centered programs and sophisticated, omni-channel outreach/solicitation campaigns aimed to drive alumni donor participation, build lifetime loyalty, and raise maximum spendable dollars for WashU’s top Annual Fund priorities. This position manages the student employees, including hiring, training and managing calling shifts for the program. The center operates Sunday – Thursday 4pm to 8pm. The working hours for this position will typically be 12:00 pm – 8:30 pm, Sunday – Thursday, with flexibility to schedule to allow attendance at meetings as necessary outside of this schedule. The Manager WashU BearLine, Direct Marketing & Participation reports to the Associate Director, Direct Marketing & Participation and will be a part of the Annual Giving Programs workflow team, working in close collaboration and partnership with Advancement Communications, Alumni & Constituent Engagement, Donor Relations, and other internal partners and external vendors. Job Description Primary Duties & Responsibilities: Student Calling Program In close collaboration with the Associate Director, Direct Marketing & Participation, Annual Giving leadership, and other internal partners, lead the day-to-day management of our Student Calling Program. Provide direct supervision of approximately 35-40 student employees within the WashU BearLine Student Calling Program. Ensure relevant recordkeeping, timekeeping, compensation, and other appropriate HR processes are followed in accordance with UA and WashU policies and standard operating procedures. Champion, and grow the internal culture of the Student Calling Program and ensure WashU’s BearLine is an exceptionally positive experience for both our students in the room and our alumni on the phone. Oversee the recruitment and retention of outstanding undergraduate and graduate students to serve as Student Ambassadors. Ambassadors are expected to define and exhibit WashU’s unique culture of philanthropy and serve as advocates for WashU and University Advancement. Coordinate Ambassador training, coaching, and performance oversight. In collaboration with student leadership, translate culture and formal responsibilities into gamified incentive and recognition programs. Partner with UA colleagues to facilitate professional development events, informational sessions on WashU updates, unique networking with/access to university leaders, etc. Coach and empower Student Ambassador Managers to carry out the program’s goals and objectives. Advise and support Shift Leaders to drive the BearLine Student Calling Program culture and to help identify our areas of growth and areas of strength. Regularly solicit and document feedback from Student Ambassadors; develop recommendations for continued growth and enhancement of the Student Calling Program. Design shift schedules and manage tasks and special projects delegated to Student Ambassadors. Seek to facilitate and fulfill staff and donor requests as directed. Ensure Operational Teams are organized and successfully carrying out their scope of work; provide insight and develop solutions to help remove roadblocks as necessary. Execute calling, texting, and other communication strategies to engage alumni and other constituents.  Phone Program Operations In partnership with the Associate Director, Direct Marketing & Participation, manage the day-to-day logistics of our calling program, including center operations. Ensure the live “call back” line is seen as the Shift Leaders’ highest priority and every call is answered in a reasonable amount of time. Document all alumni/donor interactions. Inform and coach Student Ambassadors on how to manage conversations related to recent campus announcements and other newsworthy events. Document and report comments as appropriate. Escalate unreported/unknown incidents to leadership for review. Process over-the-phone gifts through the calling program software. Ensure donor outreach for various follow-up activities are completed timely and accurately. Provide enhanced coaching and oversight to Student Ambassadors that are assigned solicitation campaigns. Ensure gifts are accurately processed or donors’ intention to give are properly documented for appropriate follow-up action. Utilize phone program software solutions to ensure all calling, texting, and video message campaigns are carried out in accordance with strategy and standard operating procedure. Support data integrity and hygiene efforts. Provide direct supervision of approximately 35-40 student employees within the WashU BearLine Student Calling Program. Ensure relevant recordkeeping, timekeeping, compensation, and other appropriate HR processes are followed in accordance with UA and WashU policies and standard operating procedures. Track activities, generate reports, and provide regular updates of various metrics pertaining to duties of this role, as needed. Collaborate with colleagues on programs and projects that overlap with those stated above. Assist, as necessary, with Annual Giving Programs and other relevant University Advancement events and campaigns. Undertake additional projects or perform other duties as assigned by the Associate Director and Senior Director, Direct Marketing & Participation, and Executive Director of Annual Giving Programs. Perform other duties as assigned. Working Conditions: Job Location/Working Conditions Normal office environment Flexible schedule and hybrid working arrangements (remote one day/week). Ability to work overtime occasionally, nights and weekend for center operations. The center operates Sunday – Thursday 4pm to 8pm. The working hours for this position will typically be 12:00 pm – 8:30 pm, Sunday – Thursday, with flexibility to schedule to allow attendance at meetings as necessary outside of this schedule. ​ Physical Effort Typically working at desk or table Repetitive wrist, hand or finger movement Ability to move to on and off-campus locations. Equipment Office equipment The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work performed by people assigned to this classification. They are not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all job duties performed by the personnel so classified. Management reserves the right to revise or amend duties at any time. Required Qualifications Education: Bachelor's degree Certifications /Professional Licenses : No specific certification/professional license is required for this position. Work Experience: Relevant Experience (2 Years) Skills: Not Applicable Driver's License: A driver's license is not required for this position. More About This Job Preferred Qualifications Experience in nonprofit advancement/development/fundraising, alumni/donor relations, or a field requiring the application of similar skills including marketing, sales, program/project management, etc. A desire to work collaboratively with colleagues. Comfortable facilitating the work of teams and individuals, including peers, students, and volunteers. Ability to relay detailed instructions and provide or solicit feedback. Committed to professional growth and remaining informed of annual giving, higher education, and other industry trends and best practices. Comfortable and nimble in learning new software systems, processes, and protocols. Familiarity with and appreciation for the mission of University Advancement programs and the activities of the department. A desire to play a meaningful role in the university’s community and to share in the community’s commitment to education and research. Willingness to accept feedback, direction, and coaching. Experience managing a phonathon program and/or serving as a student caller is highly desirable. Experience using database, website/online form solutions, and other software platforms. Familiarity with VanillaSoft, VidYard, Workday, or ClickUp. Understanding or awareness of nonprofit operations, annual giving fundraising, and direct marketing/persuasive marketing and communication strategies. This position is located in St. Louis, MO and the targeted hiring range for this position is $53,100 - $62,000. Preferred Qualifications Education: No additional education unless stated elsewhere in the job posting. Certifications /Professional Licenses : No additional certification/professional licenses unless stated elsewhere in the job posting. Work Experience: No additional work experience unless stated elsewhere in the job posting. Skills: Analytical Acumen, Blackbaud, Client Relationship Management, Client Vendor Relationship, Communication, Complex Network, Confidentiality, Conflict Resolution, Critical Thinking, Customer Service, Detail-Oriented, Learning Quickly, Marketing Campaigns, Metrics Analysis, Microsoft Office, Nonprofit Fundraising, Professional Integrity, Project Management, Salesforce System, Smartsheet Grade G11 Salary Range $53,100.00 - $90,600.00 / Annually The salary range reflects base salaries paid for positions in a given job grade across the University. Individual rates within the range will be determined by factors including one's qualifications and performance, equity with others in the department, market rates for positions within the same grade and department budget. Questions For frequently asked questions about the application process, please refer to our External Applicant FAQ . Accommodation If you are unable to use our online application system and would like an accommodation, please email   CandidateQuestions@wustl.edu   or call the dedicated accommodation inquiry number at 314-935-1149 and leave a voicemail with the nature of your request. All qualified individuals must be able to perform the essential functions of the position satisfactorily and, if requested, reasonable accommodations will be made to enable employees with disabilities to perform the essential functions of their job, absent undue hardship. Pre-Employment Screening All external candidates receiving an offer for employment will be required to submit to pre-employment screening for this position. The screenings will include criminal background check and, as applicable for the position, other background checks, drug screen, an employment and education or licensure/certification verification, physical examination, certain vaccinations and/or governmental registry checks. All offers are contingent upon successful completion of required screening. Benefits Statement Personal Up to 22 days of vacation, 10 recognized holidays, and sick time. Competitive health insurance packages with priority appointments and lower copays/coinsurance. Take advantage of our free Metro transit U-Pass for eligible employees. WashU provides eligible employees with a defined contribution (403(b)) Retirement Savings Plan, which combines employee contributions and university contributions starting at 7%. Wellness Wellness challenges, annual health screenings, mental health resources, mindfulness programs and courses, employee assistance program (EAP), financial resources, access to dietitians, and more! Family We offer 4 weeks of caregiver leave to bond with your new child. Family care resources are also available for your continued childcare needs. Need adult care? We’ve got you covered. WashU covers the cost of tuition for you and your family, including dependent undergraduate-level college tuition up to 100% at WashU and 40% elsewhere after seven years with us. For policies, detailed benefits, and eligibility, please visit: https://hr.wustl.edu/benefits/ EEO Statement Washington University in St. Louis is committed to the principles and practices of equal employment opportunity. It is the University’s policy to provide equal opportunity and access to persons in all job titles without regard to race, ethnicity, color, national origin, citizenship (where prohibited by federal law), age, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, disability, protected veteran status, or genetic information."
